{"context":"- v1.x verification is structural checks (files exist) supplied by adapters; v2 verification is lifecycle-conformity verification defined by the standard and enforced by the engine — thick conventions (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §2.2).\n- Convention depth is the product; current thickness is ~20% of what the domain requires (Review 19 §3.3).\n- Verification is the proof mechanism of the standard (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §3 A8).\n- The Verification part of a standard carries framework verification rules: structural checks and lifecycle-conformity checks (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §5.4). Source: ADR-033-verification-standard-generalization.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"- Verification checks expand from structural checks (files exist) to **lifecycle-conformity checks**: shared-resource wiring, migration timing relative to promotion, queue restart, rollback behavior (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §9 EPIC-018; Review 19 §3.3).\n- Verification rules are **standard-supplied content against the verification contract**; the verification contract itself belongs to the delivery lifecycle specification (Core-owned).\n- The Verification part of a standard (§5.4) carries both structural and lifecycle-conformity rules.\n- **Gates remain mandatory and unskippable** (verification before trust — ANVIL_MANIFESTO §3.2, §10 design principle 4); verification remains the proof mechanism of the standard.\n- Convention thickness is the standard's responsibility to deliver, validated at","consequences":"- **Positive:** thick, verifiable conventions; evidence stays re-checkable (ANVIL_MANIFESTO §3.2).\n- **Positive:** verification depth becomes measurable and gated by Review 20 §2.4 gate 2 (demonstrated convention depth).\n- **Negative:** standard authoring burden grows — verification coverage and tests are part of the standard's obligations (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §5.4, §5.6).\n- **Neutral:** the verification engine in Core remains an engine, not content (`internal/inspection` — ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §6.1).","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Keep structural checks only.** Rejected: the convention stays thin, and convention depth is the product (Review 19 §2.3, §3.3; Review 20 §2.4 gate 2).\n- **Core defines lifecycle-conformity rules.** Rejected: framework knowledge belongs outside Core (ANVIL_MANIFESTO §3.5); Core is framework-free by proof (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §2.2).\n- **Standards own the verification contract.** Rejected: contracts belong to the delivery lifecycle specification, which is Core-governed (ANVIL_V"}